    {
      "@id": "ihh:MUI-F-0053",
      "@type": "Claim",
      "text": "In 1896, on August 5, Muir and his brother David registered to vote in Contra Costa County.",
      "about": {
        "@id": "ihh:person-muir"
      },
      "ihh:category": "citizenship",
      "ihh:eventDate": "1896",
      "ihh:place": "Contra Costa County",
      "ihh:status": "single-source",
      "ihh:primaryDocument": false,
      "ihh:isDirectQuote": false,
      "citation": [
        {
          "@id": "ihh:MUI-S-0024"
        }
      ],
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": [
        {
          "@id": "ihh:MUI-S-0024"
        }
      ],
      "ihh:evidence": [
        {
          "ihh:source": {
            "@id": "ihh:MUI-S-0024"
          },
          "ihh:quote": "1896\nApril 21 : John Muir turns 58 years old\nJoins the U.S. Forestry Commission in July, chaired by Charles Sargent, on a survey of the forests of Yellowstone, the Black Hills in South Dakota, the Big Horn Mountains in Wyoming, and forests in Montana, Idaho, Oregon, and Washington.\nVisits Portland, Oregon, again in 1896, 1899, and 1908. Meets with L.L. Hawkins, William Steel, and other members of the Mazamas mountaineering club. By that time the Mazamas and the Sierra Club were working together on numerous conservation activities.\nLater surveys the Cascades, the Santa Lucia coast ranges, the mountains of southern California, the Grand Canyon and the southern Sierra Nevada.\nFifth trip to Alaska, with Henry Fairfield Osborn\nMuir feels a strong premonition about the health of his mother; travels east to Wisconsin, whereupon on June 23 : Muir's mother, Ann Gilrye Muir, dies.\nReceives Honorary A.M degree from Harvard University, 1896.\nJune 26: John Muir visits John Burroughs at Hyde Park; their second meeting. The next day Burroughs writes a journal entry about John Muir and their meeting.\nMuir makes third attempt to buy land at Fountain Lake for preservation\nAugust 5: John, along with his brother David, register to vote in Contra Costa County, stating his occupation as \"geologist\" and his basis of citizenship \"by virtue of father's naturalization.\"",
          "ihh:locator": "1896"
        }
      ],
      "ihh:extractor": {
        "ihh:model": "claude:claude-opus-5-5",
