      "ihh:notes": "Round 9 records (research R9-M-MUI-008, 2026-10-29). Chapter: muir/legacy. Shows: The item headed \"John Muir dies\" in its column; OCR: \"Los Angeles, Dec. 24.-John Muir, one of America's greatest naturalists, father of the Sequoia and Yosemite national parks' and discoverer of Muir's glacier in Alaska, died today in Los Angeles.\" Holder date: 1914-12-24. Resolution: crop region 1500,3900,1900,1900 of the page (IIIF https://tile.loc.gov/image-services/iiif/service:ndnp:curiv:batch_curiv_mesquite_ver02:data:sn92070146:00414189453:1914122401:1021/1500,3900,1900,1900/full/0/default.jpg). Preview previews/mui-legacy-ivpress-1914-12-24-p1.jpg sha256 86d07ed55b71a0b14c7b38db47f0360511d0570a2b36d7e0cd6fa26a8c5a0a5c. Supports facts: MUI-F-0081. Rights basis: Pre-1931 US newspaper: public domain by date (owner rule: pre-1931 US publications); LoC Chronicling America statement. Do not narrate 'father of the Sequoia and Yosemite national parks' (newspaper wording, not a verified fact). Page snapshot snapshots/loc-sn92070146_1914-12-24_ed-1_sp_1.json.\nrights_url set on 2026-10-30 to the page record itself (was https://www.loc.gov/homepage/legal.html, which does not carry the Chronicling America statement; the statement is on the page record, as on the round-7 pages)."
